{"reply":"In Chapter 2, Alice’s physical instability immediately becomes a question of identity: after wondering whether she has been “changed,” she asks, “Who in the world am I?” Her changing body makes her usual tests of identity—her memories, knowledge, voice, and resemblance to other children—feel unreliable.

Chapter 5 makes the connection explicit. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_”, Alice answers that she has “been changed several times,” then says, “I’m not myself,” because “being so many different sizes in a day is very confusing.” Her size changes do not merely happen to her; they disrupt the continuity by which she recognizes herself. At the same time, her uncertainty about who she is makes each new physical change feel like evidence that she has become someone else.

So the two problems reinforce each other in a loop: bodily change undermines identity, and unstable identity makes bodily change more disorienting. The Caterpillar’s repeated question leaves that loop unresolved rather than giving Alice a fixed definition of herself. What seems most important to you here: that Alice cannot control her body, or that she cannot find a stable way to describe who she is?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch02-ln0327-0360","source_location":"Chapter 2 — The Pool of Tears, source lines 327-360","exact_quote":"“Who in the world am I? Ah, _that’s_ the great puzzle!”","supported_claims":["In Chapter 2, Alice’s physical instability immediately becomes a question of identity: after wondering whether she has been “changed,” she asks, “Who in the world am I?”","Her changing body makes her usual tests of identity—her memories, knowledge, voice, and resemblance to other children—feel unreliable."]},{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch05-ln0960-1016","source_location":"Chapter 5 — Advice from a Caterpillar, source lines 960-1016","exact_quote":"“Who are _you?_”","supported_claims":["Chapter 5 makes the connection explicit. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_”, Alice answers that she has “been changed several times,” then says, “I’m not myself,” because “being so many different sizes in a day is very confusing.”","Her size changes do not merely happen to her; they disrupt the continuity by which she recognizes herself.","At the same time, her uncertainty about who she is makes each new physical change feel like evidence that she has become someone else.","The Caterpillar’s repeated question leaves that loop unresolved rather than giving Alice a fixed definition of herself."]}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}
